1
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:06,990
- Hey.     - Hey.

2
00:00:07,170 --> 00:00:09,330
- So how was Joan?     - Broke up with her.

3
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:10,910
Oh, why?

4
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:13,870
Don't tell me. Because of the big nostril thing?

5
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:16,840
They were huge.

6
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:20,050
When she sneezed, bats flew out of them.

7
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:22,850
Come on, they were not that huge.

8
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:26,390
I'm telling you, she leaned back, I could see her brain.

9
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:30,060
How many women will you reject...

10
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:32,560
over the most superficial, insignificant things?

11
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:35,520
Hold it. Hold it. I gotta side with Chandler on this one.

12
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:38,320
When I first moved to the city, I went out with this girl.

13
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:40,990
Really hot. Great kisser...

14
00:00:41,290 --> 00:00:44,370
but she had the biggest Adam's apple.

15
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:46,580
Drove me nuts.

16
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:50,790
- You or me?     - I got it.

17
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,620
Joey, women don't have Adam's apples.

18
00:01:01,220 --> 00:01:03,510
Heh, heh. You guys are messing with me, right?

19
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:07,310
Yeah. We are.

20
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,060
That's a good one. For a second there, I was like, whoa!

21
00:01:18,390 --> 00:01:22,920
∮ So no one told you life was gonna be this way ∮

22
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:27,630
∮ Your job's a joke, you're broke, your love life's D.O.A. ∮

23
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:32,350
∮ It's like you're always stuck in second gear ∮

24
00:01:32,810 --> 00:01:34,910
∮ When it hasn't been your day, ∮

25
00:01:34,910 --> 00:01:37,910
∮ your week, your month, or even your year ∮

26
00:01:37,910 --> 00:01:41,870
∮ but I'll be there for you ∮

27
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:43,700
∮ When the rain starts to pour ∮

28
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:46,820
∮ I'll be there for you ∮

29
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:48,660
∮ Like I've been there before ∮

30
00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:51,670
∮ I'll be there for you ∮

31
00:01:51,670 --> 00:01:55,330
∮ 'Cause you're there for me too ∮

32
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:04,110
So you name one woman that you broke up with for an actual real reason.

33
00:02:06,330 --> 00:02:07,490
Maureen Rasillo.

34
00:02:07,710 --> 00:02:11,370
Because she doesn't hate Yanni is not a real reason.

35
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:17,380
Hello, Mr. Heckles.

36
00:02:18,510 --> 00:02:19,670
You're doing it again.

37
00:02:20,050 --> 00:02:21,340
We're not doing anything.

38
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:24,510
You're stomping. It's disturbing my birds.

39
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:28,930
You don't have birds.

40
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:31,350
I could have birds.

41
00:02:32,310 --> 00:02:35,180
- Okay, we'll try to keep it down.     - Thank you.

42
00:02:36,530 --> 00:02:38,520
I'm going to rejoin my dinner party.

43
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:41,110
All right, bye-bye.

44
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:45,700
Okay, Janice. You gotta give me Janice. That wasn't about being picky.

45
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:48,150
We'll give you Janice.

46
00:02:48,330 --> 00:02:50,160
I miss Janice, though.

47
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:52,490
Hello, Chandler Bing!

48
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:59,210
- Oh, my God!     - Oh, Chandler!

49
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:02,590
Now! Now!

50
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:06,300
That's it.

51
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:09,600
There! Faster!

52
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:20,440
Ugh. Stop with the broom! We're not making noise!

53
00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:31,620
We won.

54
00:03:31,830 --> 00:03:33,320
- We won! Whoo!     - Whoo!

55
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:38,120
Mr. Heckles.

56
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:44,750
- How did this happen?     - He was sweeping.

57
00:03:45,050 --> 00:03:47,210
They found a broom in his hand.

58
00:03:50,390 --> 00:03:52,050
- Oh, dear God. That's terrible.     - I know.

59
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:54,430
I was sweeping yesterday.

60
00:03:54,730 --> 00:03:56,440
- It could've been me.     - Yeah.

61
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:00,190
- Sure.     - Sweeping. You never know.

62
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:01,560
Never know.

63
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:10,030
Okay, it's very faint, but I can still sense him in the building.

64
00:04:10,750 --> 00:04:13,530
Go into the light, Mr. Heckles!

65
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:18,370
Okay, Phoebe.

66
00:04:19,130 --> 00:04:21,250
I'm sorry, but sometimes they need help.

67
00:04:22,670 --> 00:04:24,590
- Yeah.     - Here we go.

68
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:27,260
That's fine, go ahead and scoff.

69
00:04:27,550 --> 00:04:29,970
There are a lot of things I don't believe in...

70
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:32,300
but that doesn't mean they're not true.

71
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:33,760
Such as?

72
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:38,600
Like crop circles or the Bermuda Triangle or evolution.

73
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:40,610
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

74
00:04:42,570 --> 00:04:44,780
What, you don't believe in evolution?

75
00:04:45,110 --> 00:04:46,270
No. Not really.

76
00:04:48,950 --> 00:04:50,440
You don't believe in evolution?

77
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:53,070
I don't know. It's just, you know...

78
00:04:53,290 --> 00:04:58,080
Monkeys, Darwin, it's a nice story. I just think it's a little too easy.

79
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:04,250
"Too easy"? Too...

80
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:07,920
The process of every living thing on this planet evolving...

81
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:12,470
over millions of years from single-celled organisms is "too easy"?

82
00:05:13,270 --> 00:05:15,260
Yeah, I just don't buy it.

83
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:20,350
Excuse me. Evolution is not for you to buy, Phoebe.

84
00:05:20,650 --> 00:05:24,440
Evolution is scientific fact, like the air we breathe...

85
00:05:24,650 --> 00:05:26,190
like gravity.

86
00:05:26,450 --> 00:05:28,690
Okay, don't get me started on gravity.

87
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:33,820
You, uh...

88
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,700
You don't believe in gravity?

89
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:41,160
Well, it's not so much that, you know, I don't believe in it.

90
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:43,290
Lately I get the feeling that...

91
00:05:43,510 --> 00:05:46,670
I'm not so much being pulled down as I am being pushed.

92
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:48,090
Pushed.

93
00:05:50,350 --> 00:05:52,680
Uh oh. It's Isaac Newton and he's pissed.

94
00:05:55,930 --> 00:05:59,180
There she is. And over there, that's the other one.

95
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:02,890
This is Mr. Buddy Boyle, Heckles' attorney. He'd like to talk to you.

96
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:04,560
What can we do for you?

97
00:06:04,730 --> 00:06:06,850
All right, kids, here's the deal.

98
00:06:07,070 --> 00:06:09,060
According my client's will...

99
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:13,870
he wants to leave all his earthly possessions...

100
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:17,870
to "the noisy girls in the apartment above mine."

101
00:06:19,370 --> 00:06:20,530
What about his family?

102
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:22,240
- He didn't have any.     - Whoa.

103
00:06:24,210 --> 00:06:26,420
Okay, so let's talk money.

104
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:29,590
All right, there was none.

105
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:31,420
Now, let's talk signing.

106
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:34,880
You be noisy girl number one. You be noisy girl number two.

107
00:06:36,470 --> 00:06:39,590
I can't believe that this whole time we thought he hated us.

108
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:43,310
Isn't it amazing how much you can touch someone's life...

109
00:06:43,610 --> 00:06:45,560
without even knowing it?

110
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:47,270
Would you look at this dump?

111
00:06:47,490 --> 00:06:49,770
He hated us! This was his final revenge!

112
00:06:50,070 --> 00:06:52,280
Ever seen so much crap!

113
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:56,110
Actually, I think this apartment sullies the good name of crap.

114
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:58,780
Check this out.

115
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:02,290
Can I have this?

116
00:07:03,670 --> 00:07:06,460
How can you not believe in evolution?

117
00:07:07,460 --> 00:07:10,420
I don't know, just don't. Look at this funky shirt!

118
00:07:11,260 --> 00:07:14,090
Pheebs, I've studied evolution my entire adult life.

119
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:17,640
And I can tell you, we've collected fossils from all over the world...

120
00:07:17,970 --> 00:07:21,340
that actually show the evolution of different species.

121
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:24,350
I mean, you can literally see them...

122
00:07:24,610 --> 00:07:28,140
evolving through time.

123
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:31,310
Really? You can actually see it?

124
00:07:31,530 --> 00:07:36,150
You bet! In the U.S., China, Africa, all over!

125
00:07:36,870 --> 00:07:38,780
See, I didn't know that.

126
00:07:39,370 --> 00:07:41,490
Well, there you go.

127
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:43,080
Huh.

128
00:07:43,290 --> 00:07:48,160
So now the real question is, who put those fossils there and why?

129
00:07:52,180 --> 00:07:55,670
Hey. Look at this. My Big Book of Grievances.

130
00:07:58,350 --> 00:07:59,840
Hey, there's me.

131
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:03,380
"April 17th, excessive noise...

132
00:08:03,650 --> 00:08:06,510
ltalian guy comes home with a date." Yeah.

133
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:08,690
Chandler, you're in here too.

134
00:08:09,190 --> 00:08:11,520
"April 18th, excessive noise...

135
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:15,480
Italian guy's gay roommate brings home dry cleaning."

136
00:08:22,410 --> 00:08:24,200
Well, that's excellent.

137
00:08:25,540 --> 00:08:26,870
Monica, look at this lamp.

138
00:08:27,090 --> 00:08:29,540
Is this tacky or what? We have to have this.

139
00:08:30,710 --> 00:08:32,880
I think we have enough regular lamps.

140
00:08:33,170 --> 00:08:35,260
What? Come on, it's not like...

141
00:08:35,510 --> 00:08:38,220
I'm asking for this girlie clock or anything.

142
00:08:38,430 --> 00:08:41,340
Which, by the way, I also think is really cool.

143
00:08:42,230 --> 00:08:44,560
Look, it doesn't go with any of my stuff.

144
00:08:45,770 --> 00:08:47,180
Well, what about my stuff?

145
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:49,730
You don't have any stuff.

146
00:08:51,070 --> 00:08:53,400
You still think of it as your apartment, don't you?

147
00:08:55,110 --> 00:08:57,200
- Mmm... No.     - Yes, you do.

148
00:08:57,450 --> 00:09:00,740
You think of it as your apartment and I just rent a room.

149
00:09:01,790 --> 00:09:03,040
Mmm...

150
00:09:03,370 --> 00:09:07,410
Okay, while you "mmm" on that, I'll go find a place for my new lamp.

151
00:09:10,750 --> 00:09:14,340
Okay, Pheebs. See how I'm making these little toys move?

152
00:09:17,470 --> 00:09:18,630
Opposable thumbs!

153
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:25,130
Without evolution, how do you explain opposable thumbs?

154
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:29,970
Maybe the Overlords needed them to steer their spacecrafts.

155
00:09:31,230 --> 00:09:32,390
Tell me you're joking.

156
00:09:32,940 --> 00:09:36,810
Look, can't we just say that you believe in something and I don't?

157
00:09:37,110 --> 00:09:40,360
- No. No, Pheebs, we can't.     - Why not?

158
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:43,860
What is this obsessive need you have to make everybody agree with you?

159
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:45,870
No, what's that all about? You know what I think?

160
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:51,380
I think maybe it's time you put Ross under the microscope.

161
00:09:56,550 --> 00:09:58,590
Is there blood coming out of my ears?

162
00:10:00,470 --> 00:10:03,130
Check it out. Heckles' high-school yearbook.

163
00:10:05,390 --> 00:10:06,680
Wow. He looks so normal.

164
00:10:06,890 --> 00:10:08,880
He's even kind of cute.

165
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:11,390
"Heckles, you crack me up in science class.

166
00:10:11,690 --> 00:10:13,680
You're the funniest kid in school."

167
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:16,230
- Funniest? Heckles?     - That's what it says.

168
00:10:17,450 --> 00:10:20,730
Wow. Heckles was voted class clown, and so was I.

169
00:10:22,620 --> 00:10:24,900
He was right. Would you listen to that?

170
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:27,410
I'd call that excessive.

171
00:10:28,210 --> 00:10:29,370
- Whoa.     - What?

172
00:10:29,540 --> 00:10:32,370
Heckles played clarinet in band, and I played clarinet.

173
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:36,130
And he was in the scale modeler's club and I was...

174
00:10:36,380 --> 00:10:39,460
Well, there was no club, but I sure thought they were cool.

175
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:42,220
So? You were both dorks.

176
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:43,960
Big deal.

177
00:10:44,140 --> 00:10:47,130
I just think it's weird, you know? Heckles and me...

178
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:49,430
Heckles and me. Me...

179
00:10:49,770 --> 00:10:50,930
Me and Heckles.

180
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:53,640
Hey, would you knock it off!

181
00:10:57,940 --> 00:10:59,110
Ah!

182
00:11:12,210 --> 00:11:13,450
Have you been here all night?

183
00:11:14,210 --> 00:11:15,500
Look at this.

184
00:11:16,130 --> 00:11:18,040
Pictures of all the women Heckles went out with.

185
00:11:18,210 --> 00:11:21,960
Look what he wrote on them. "Vivian, too tall."

186
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:23,830
"Madge, big gums."

187
00:11:24,890 --> 00:11:26,050
"Too loud."

188
00:11:26,390 --> 00:11:28,470
"Too smart." "Makes noise when she eats."

189
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:30,300
This is me.

190
00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:32,390
This is what I do.

191
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:36,520
I'm gonna end up alone, just like he did.

192
00:11:36,730 --> 00:11:38,390
Chandler, Heckles was a nut case.

193
00:11:38,650 --> 00:11:40,560
Our trains are on the same track.

194
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:44,230
I'm coming up 30 years behind him, but the stops are all the same.

195
00:11:44,570 --> 00:11:46,160
Bitter Town...

196
00:11:46,580 --> 00:11:49,160
Alone-ville, Hermit Junction!

197
00:11:50,750 --> 00:11:53,080
You know what we gotta do? Get you out of here.

198
00:11:53,330 --> 00:11:54,990
Come on, I'll buy you breakfast.

199
00:11:55,250 --> 00:11:58,080
What if I never find somebody? Or worse, what if I found her...

200
00:11:58,340 --> 00:12:02,210
but I dumped her because she pronounces it "supposably"?

201
00:12:02,930 --> 00:12:05,920
Chandler, come on, you're gonna find somebody.

202
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:08,040
How do you know that? How?

203
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:10,930
I don't know. I'm just trying to help you out.

204
00:12:12,060 --> 00:12:13,930
You guys will all get married...

205
00:12:14,190 --> 00:12:15,600
and I'll end up alone.

206
00:12:16,440 --> 00:12:18,050
Will you promise me something?

207
00:12:18,270 --> 00:12:21,610
When you're married, will you invite me over for holidays?

208
00:12:22,110 --> 00:12:23,770
I don't know what we'll be doing.

209
00:12:26,780 --> 00:12:29,950
I mean, what if we're over at her folks' place?

210
00:12:32,250 --> 00:12:34,280
Yeah, I understand.

211
00:12:37,540 --> 00:12:39,960
You can come over and watch the Super Bowl.

212
00:12:40,130 --> 00:12:42,090
Every year, all right?

213
00:12:43,300 --> 00:12:46,630
You know what? I'm not gonna end up like this.

214
00:12:46,930 --> 00:12:48,760
I'll see you, man.

215
00:12:53,060 --> 00:12:54,230
Jeez.

216
00:12:58,310 --> 00:12:59,770
"Supposably."

217
00:13:02,490 --> 00:13:03,940
Supposably.

218
00:13:06,570 --> 00:13:08,810
Did they go to the zoo?

219
00:13:08,990 --> 00:13:11,230
Supposably.

220
00:13:23,800 --> 00:13:26,130
Hi. It's me.

221
00:13:26,590 --> 00:13:30,010
Oh, my God!

222
00:13:31,930 --> 00:13:34,140
Janice? You called Janice?

223
00:13:35,810 --> 00:13:36,970
Yes. Janice.

224
00:13:37,230 --> 00:13:39,140
Why is that difficult to comprehend?

225
00:13:39,400 --> 00:13:41,600
You remember Janice, right?

226
00:13:42,780 --> 00:13:47,310
Yes. She was smart, she was pretty and she honestly cared about me.

227
00:13:47,570 --> 00:13:50,230
Janice is my last chance to have somebody.

228
00:13:51,780 --> 00:13:53,570
Hello.

229
00:13:56,500 --> 00:13:59,330
Oh, my God!

230
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:03,160
Jeez, look how fat she got.

231
00:14:05,170 --> 00:14:08,840
- Hey, it's everybody.     - Hi, Janice.

232
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:10,300
Janice, you're...

233
00:14:10,510 --> 00:14:12,500
Yes, I am.

234
00:14:13,350 --> 00:14:15,180
- Is it...?     - Is it yours? Ha!

235
00:14:15,430 --> 00:14:17,470
You wish, Chandler Bing.

236
00:14:18,190 --> 00:14:22,100
You are looking at a married lady now.

237
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:23,480
Oh.

238
00:14:23,650 --> 00:14:25,110
Yeah.

239
00:14:26,780 --> 00:14:28,610
Congratulations.

240
00:14:28,860 --> 00:14:31,070
Aw, sweetie, I'm sorry.

241
00:14:31,490 --> 00:14:33,360
You couldn't tell me on the phone?

242
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:36,580
And what? Miss the expression on your face? Oh, no.

243
00:14:36,790 --> 00:14:39,580
Janice likes to have her fun.

244
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:53,890
You know what we haven't played in a while?

245
00:14:54,060 --> 00:14:56,510
- What?     - Hide the lamp.

246
00:14:58,480 --> 00:15:00,260
Monica, let it go.

247
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:03,440
Did you know I'm allergic to shellfish?

248
00:15:03,650 --> 00:15:06,730
Then you'll just have to eat the other lamps.

249
00:15:09,860 --> 00:15:12,480
Uh oh. It's scary scientist man.

250
00:15:15,160 --> 00:15:16,900
Okay, Phoebe, this is it.

251
00:15:17,120 --> 00:15:20,830
In this briefcase I carry actual scientific facts.

252
00:15:21,080 --> 00:15:23,790
A briefcase of facts, if you will.

253
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:31,000
Some of these fossils are over 200 million years old.

254
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:34,460
Okay, look, before you even start, I'm not denying evolution.

255
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:36,800
It's just one of the possibilities.

256
00:15:37,010 --> 00:15:38,970
It's the only possibility.

257
00:15:39,230 --> 00:15:42,590
Ross, could you just open your mind, like, this much? Okay?

258
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:48,560
Wasn't there a time when the brightest minds in the world believe the Earth was flat?

259
00:15:49,490 --> 00:15:54,070
And up till 50 years ago, you all thought the atom was the smallest thing...

260
00:15:54,320 --> 00:15:58,030
until you split it open and this whole mess of crap came out.

261
00:16:00,370 --> 00:16:04,540
Now, are you telling me that you are so unbelievably arrogant...

262
00:16:04,830 --> 00:16:08,620
that you can't admit that there's a teeny, tiny possibility...

263
00:16:08,840 --> 00:16:10,830
that you could be wrong about this?

264
00:16:17,640 --> 00:16:19,550
There might be...

265
00:16:20,850 --> 00:16:22,680
a teeny...

266
00:16:23,980 --> 00:16:25,180
tiny...

267
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:28,470
possibility.

268
00:16:30,230 --> 00:16:32,440
I can't believe you caved.

269
00:16:34,410 --> 00:16:38,690
- What?     - You just abandoned your whole belief system!

270
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:43,370
Before, I didn't agree with you, but at least I respected you.

271
00:16:43,790 --> 00:16:45,030
But...

272
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:47,710
How are you gonna go into work tomorrow?

273
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:50,660
How are you going to face the other science guys?

274
00:16:50,880 --> 00:16:53,710
How are you going to face yourself? Oh...

275
00:17:10,270 --> 00:17:13,440
- That was fun. So who's hungry?     - I am.

276
00:17:13,650 --> 00:17:15,860
Me too. Let me just get my coat.

277
00:17:23,960 --> 00:17:27,070
- What happened?     - Okay. All right.

278
00:17:27,250 --> 00:17:28,660
It was an accident, I swear.

279
00:17:28,920 --> 00:17:32,830
I was putting on my jacket and the thing and the lamp and it broke.

280
00:17:33,090 --> 00:17:36,460
Oh, please, Monica! You've always hated my lamp!

281
00:17:36,720 --> 00:17:40,930
And now all of a sudden, it's just magically, it's just broken?

282
00:17:41,180 --> 00:17:42,390
Phoebe, tell her?

283
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:45,930
I didn't see it, because I was putting on my jacket.

284
00:17:46,140 --> 00:17:47,470
But I want to believe you.

285
00:17:51,940 --> 00:17:55,850
Hey, Chandler. Monica just broke my seashell lamp.

286
00:17:56,400 --> 00:17:57,560
Neat.

287
00:17:57,780 --> 00:17:59,650
I'm gonna die alone!

288
00:18:01,990 --> 00:18:03,820
Okay, you win.

289
00:18:05,040 --> 00:18:07,320
Chandler, you are not gonna die alone.

290
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:09,820
Janice was my safety net, okay?

291
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:11,700
And now I have to get a snake.

292
00:18:14,010 --> 00:18:15,170
Uh huh. Why is that?

293
00:18:15,340 --> 00:18:18,500
If I'm gonna be an old, lonely man, I'm gonna need a thing.

294
00:18:18,760 --> 00:18:20,370
You know, a hook.

295
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:23,630
Like that guy on the subway who eats his own face.

296
00:18:24,310 --> 00:18:26,340
So I figure I'll be "Crazy Man With A Snake."

297
00:18:26,600 --> 00:18:27,840
Crazy Snake Man.

298
00:18:28,100 --> 00:18:30,090
Then I'll get more snakes, call them my babies.

299
00:18:30,350 --> 00:18:32,470
Kids won't walk past my place, they will run!

300
00:18:32,770 --> 00:18:35,890
"Run away from Crazy Snake Man," they'll shout!

301
00:18:39,450 --> 00:18:41,940
You've got to get over this. Okay?

302
00:18:42,410 --> 00:18:43,770
You won't end up alone.

303
00:18:44,040 --> 00:18:45,570
Of course I am.

304
00:18:45,830 --> 00:18:49,570
I reject anyone who's crazy enough to actually go out with me...

305
00:18:49,790 --> 00:18:53,450
and then I bitch that there aren't any great women out there.

306
00:18:54,710 --> 00:18:55,950
Chandler

307
00:18:56,130 --> 00:18:57,420
You have just described...

308
00:18:57,630 --> 00:19:01,250
virtually every man that we've ever gone out with.

309
00:19:01,470 --> 00:19:02,930
Yeah.

310
00:19:03,220 --> 00:19:05,340
You are not a freak. You're a guy.

311
00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:10,180
She's right. You're no different than the rest.

312
00:19:10,440 --> 00:19:12,600
Wait a minute. Wait a minute. Yes, he is.

313
00:19:12,810 --> 00:19:15,850
- You are totally different.     - In a bad way?

314
00:19:16,110 --> 00:19:17,850
No, honey, in a wonderful way.

315
00:19:18,110 --> 00:19:19,850
You know what you want now.

316
00:19:20,110 --> 00:19:21,600
Most guys don't even have a clue.

317
00:19:21,860 --> 00:19:25,650
You're ready to take risks, be vulnerable and intimate with someone.

318
00:19:25,950 --> 00:19:27,440
You're not gonna end up alone.

319
00:19:27,750 --> 00:19:33,830
You called Janice. That's how much you wanted to be with someone!

320
00:19:35,630 --> 00:19:37,490
- You've made it.     - You're there.

321
00:19:37,710 --> 00:19:40,130
You are ready to make a commitment!

322
00:19:40,380 --> 00:19:42,620
Whoa! Don't know about that.

323
00:19:49,980 --> 00:19:51,140
Wow. What you got there?

324
00:19:51,310 --> 00:19:54,470
Something else that's not yours that you can break?

325
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:57,230
No.

326
00:19:57,400 --> 00:20:00,390
I know you like this and I want you to have it.

327
00:20:00,650 --> 00:20:02,640
It'll look good in our apartment.

328
00:20:03,820 --> 00:20:05,650
Thank you.

329
00:20:11,710 --> 00:20:13,320
That's fine.

330
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:15,660
- Hey.     - Hey.

331
00:20:15,840 --> 00:20:19,370
You'll all be pleased to know that I have a date tomorrow night.

332
00:20:20,170 --> 00:20:22,000
This woman Allison from work. She is great.

333
00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:25,380
She's pretty, she's smart. And I've been...

334
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:28,920
holding off asking her out in the past, because she has a...

335
00:20:30,020 --> 00:20:31,550
unusually large head.

336
00:20:33,060 --> 00:20:36,220
But I'm not going to let that stuff hang me up anymore. All right.

337
00:20:36,860 --> 00:20:38,220
Look at me. I'm growing.

338
00:20:40,030 --> 00:20:42,690
Hey, you can't recycle yearbooks, can you?

339
00:20:43,530 --> 00:20:44,690
I'll take that.

340
00:20:44,910 --> 00:20:46,820
- You want his yearbook?     - Yeah. Yeah.

341
00:20:46,990 --> 00:20:50,700
Some people said nice things about him. Somebody should have it.

342
00:20:50,910 --> 00:20:53,370
Gosh, this is so weird.

343
00:20:53,710 --> 00:20:56,910
His whole life was in this apartment and now it's gone.

344
00:20:58,210 --> 00:21:02,540
I think it would be nice if we just took a few moments for Mr. Heckles.

345
00:21:03,630 --> 00:21:07,470
I mean, he was kind of a pain. He was, but he was a person.

346
00:21:09,300 --> 00:21:11,100
What?

347
00:21:14,310 --> 00:21:15,930
You're all going to hell.

348
00:21:16,230 --> 00:21:18,390
It's really not that big.

349
00:21:21,570 --> 00:21:23,810
- Taking that with you, huh?     - Oh, yeah.

350
00:21:29,950 --> 00:21:31,570
Hey, you coming?

351
00:21:32,040 --> 00:21:33,320
Yeah, in a second.

352
00:21:49,510 --> 00:21:51,500
Goodbye, Mr. Heckles.

353
00:21:54,810 --> 00:21:56,970
We'll try to keep it down.

354
00:22:07,910 --> 00:22:09,520
My major was useless.

355
00:22:09,700 --> 00:22:13,820
How often do you look in the classifieds and see "Philosopher Wanted"?

356
00:22:14,040 --> 00:22:15,530
Heh, heh. Sure.

357
00:22:15,700 --> 00:22:17,950
My God, that's a big head!

358
00:22:19,880 --> 00:22:23,710
It didn't look this big in the office. Maybe it's the lighting.

359
00:22:23,880 --> 00:22:27,120
My head must look like a golf ball at work.

360
00:22:28,220 --> 00:22:31,960
Don't get hung up on it. Quick, quick. List five things you like about her.

361
00:22:32,260 --> 00:22:34,220
Nice smile, good dresser.

362
00:22:34,390 --> 00:22:36,930
Big head, big head, big head!

